## What the law says

On **1 October 2025**, the applicable law was the **Residential Tenancies Act 1997, Authorised Version No. 110**, incorporating amendments as at **6 August 2025**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

- A property must be advertised or offered for rent at a **fixed amount**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The rental provider or agent must not **solicit or invite** an offer above the advertised rent. Breaching this rule is an offence carrying up to **60 penalty units for a natural person or 300 penalty units for a body corporate**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The Act does **not expressly prohibit an applicant from voluntarily offering more** without being invited to do so. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- Advertising that makes a **false or misleading representation about the rent** is also prohibited, with the same maximum penalties. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The documents do not say that merely relisting the property at a higher fixed rent is automatically unlawful. The key questions are whether the earlier price was false or misleading, or whether the provider or agent invited applicants to bid above it. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The false statement that you withdrew could help show what occurred, but the retrieved Act does not itself create a specific remedy merely because an application was incorrectly marked “withdrawn.”[[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

## What you can do or ask

You can report the matter to the **Director of Consumer Affairs Victoria** and ask for investigation and enforcement, particularly if you believe the agent invited higher bids or used misleading rent advertising. The Director monitors compliance; only the Director, a person authorised by the Director, or a police officer may prosecute an offence under the Act. [[2]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

VCAT has jurisdiction over matters concerning a **proposed residential rental agreement** and may restrain breaches or order compensation, but the retrieved provisions do not clearly confirm that an unsuccessful applicant can personally bring a rental-bidding claim in these circumstances. A non-party may apply with VCAT’s leave only in relation to a residential rental agreement and must show sufficient interest and personal involvement. [[2]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

### Save as evidence

The retrieved documents contain no formal evidence checklist. To establish the elements above, preserve:

- the original advertisement showing the property, date and fixed rent;
- screenshots of every later listing showing the higher rent, including dates and website details;
- your submitted application and confirmation showing the rent you offered;
- the email saying you withdrew, with full headers and timestamp;
- your reply stating that you did not withdraw;
- all emails, texts, portal messages and notes of calls concerning price or competing offers;
- any message inviting you or another applicant to offer more;
- the name of anyone who heard such an invitation and exactly what they were told; and
- a dated timeline of the advertisement, application, withdrawal email and relisting.

This material is relevant because the decisive issues are the advertised fixed amount, any invitation to offer more, and whether the rent advertising was false or misleading. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

## What the law says

On **1 October 2025**, the relevant document was the **Residential Tenancies Act 1997, Authorised Version No. 110**, incorporating amendments as at **6 August 2025**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

- A property must be advertised or offered for rent at a **fixed amount**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The rental provider or agent must not **solicit or invite** an offer higher than the advertised rent. Breach carries a maximum penalty of **60 penalty units for a natural person or 300 penalty units for a body corporate**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The Act does **not expressly prohibit an applicant from voluntarily offering more**. The key question is whether the provider or agent solicited or invited that higher offer. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- False or misleading representations about the advertised rent are also prohibited in the circumstances covered by section 30G. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- Relisting at a higher fixed rent is not, by itself, expressly prohibited. But the sequence may support an allegation that the original rent advertisement was false or misleading, or that higher offers were invited. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The retrieved Act does not specifically make a false “you withdrew” email a separate offence. Section 30E concerns misleading conduct used to **induce someone to enter an agreement**, so it does not clearly cover your situation. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

## What you can do or ask

You can report the matter to the **Director of Consumer Affairs Victoria**, whose statutory functions include monitoring compliance with the Act. [[3]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf) Ask the agent or provider in writing:

1. to correct the statement that you withdrew;
2. to confirm whether your application was considered at the advertised rent;
3. whether any applicant was invited or encouraged to offer more; and
4. why the property was relisted at a higher amount.

The retrieved documents do not clearly give an unsuccessful applicant a direct VCAT application specifically for a breach of sections 30F or 30G. Although VCAT has jurisdiction over matters involving a **proposed residential rental agreement**, the provisions about who may apply do not expressly give a prospective applicant a specific application right for rental bidding. [[3]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

## Evidence to save

The Act does not provide a specific evidence checklist. To preserve material relevant to whether the rent was fixed, whether higher offers were invited, and whether the advertising was misleading, save:

- the original advertisement showing the address, advertised rent and date;
- your complete application and submission confirmation;
- the email saying you withdrew, including headers and timestamp;
- your written reply stating that you did not withdraw;
- screenshots of the later higher-priced listing, with date and platform;
- every email, text or application-screen message mentioning higher offers, “best offers”, competition or changing the offered rent;
- any direct statement from the other applicant, preferably in writing, about being invited to offer more; and
- a dated timeline of the advertisement, application, withdrawal email and relisting.

A rumour that somebody voluntarily offered more is not enough by itself to show the prohibited conduct: evidence that the provider or agent **solicited or invited** the higher offer would directly address section 30F(3). [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
